// Why a value could not be turned into text.
//
// `TextError` next door answers a different question. It is this package's storage-and-validation status, it has a
// `None` case, and callers test it with `IsOk`. That shape suits an operation whose answer is "did this work", but
// it is the wrong shape for a protocol that returns `Result`: a `Result::Error` carrying `TextError::None` would be
// a failure that says nothing failed, and nothing prevents one being constructed.
//
// So `FormatError` is failure-only. Every case denotes a failure, there is no `None`, `Ok` or `Success` case, and
// success lives exclusively in `Result::Success`. The same rule applies one level down: `TextFailureKind` carries
// the four ways text handling actually fails and cannot express the fifth thing `TextError` can say.
//
// The categories are split by who is at fault, because that is what a caller can act on. A malformed specification
// or an argument-count mismatch is the call site's mistake and carries where. An unsupported request or a value out
// of range is a mismatch between a value and what was asked of it. Insufficient capacity is the destination being
// too small. A writer failure is the destination itself failing, and its details stay in the adapter that knows
// them: this package has no vocabulary for a broken pipe and does not pretend to.

import Core::{ Result, Unit };

/// The ways text handling fails, with no case for success.
///
/// A deliberate narrowing of `TextError`: the same four failures, minus its `None`. That absence is the point, since
/// this is what a `FormatError::TextFailure` carries and a failure payload must not be able to say "nothing went
/// wrong".
///
/// Copy, carrying no payload and owning nothing.
///
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/text-failure-kind
pub variant TextFailureKind {
    /// The allocator could not supply the storage a write needed.
    OutOfMemory,
    /// A length or offset computation passed what a `uint` can hold.
    LengthOverflow,
    /// The bytes offered were not well-formed UTF-8.
    InvalidUtf8,
    /// A byte offset that is not where a character starts, and so not a place text may be cut.
    NotABoundary
}

/// Why a formatting operation did not produce the text it was asked for.
///
/// Failure-only: every case is a failure, and success is `Result::Success` rather than a case here. Which case
/// arises says who must fix it, so the categories are not interchangeable and a writer may not invent the ones that
/// belong to the parser or the value.
///
/// Copy, and cheap to return: the widest payload is a pair of counts.
///
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/format-error
pub variant FormatError {
    /// The pattern or specification was malformed, at the zero-based UTF-8 byte offset carried here. The offset is
    /// into the text that was being parsed, and end-of-input is that text's length.
    InvalidSpecification(uint),
    /// The style or flag asked for is not one this value supports. A precision on a boolean, or a base on a string,
    /// arrives here rather than being ignored.
    UnsupportedRequest,
    /// A pattern's placeholders and the arguments supplied did not agree: expected first, supplied second.
    ArgumentCountMismatch(uint, uint),
    /// The destination is bounded and cannot accept the whole write. Nothing partial is left behind by a writer
    /// that promises otherwise; see the writer's own contract.
    InsufficientCapacity,
    /// The value is valid but cannot be represented in the form requested, such as a timestamp outside the
    /// calendar range a format covers.
    ValueOutOfRange,
    /// Text handling failed, for the reason carried. This is where an allocation failure or rejected UTF-8 arrives.
    TextFailure(TextFailureKind),
    /// The destination failed on its own terms. Its details stay with the adapter that produced it, which is the
    /// only thing that knows what a failure of that destination means.
    WriterFailure
}

extend FormatError {
    /// Whether this failure came from the destination rather than from the text or the request.
    ///
    /// Useful at a boundary that must tell an unusable destination apart from a request that was wrong: the first is
    /// worth retrying elsewhere and the second never is.
    ///
    /// Borrows the error and leaves it untouched. Cannot fail; runs in constant time and allocates nothing.
    ///
    /// @returns true for `WriterFailure`, false for every other case
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/format-error/is-writer-failure
    pub func IsWriterFailure(self: &FormatError) -> bool {
        return match self {
            .WriterFailure => true,
            else => false
        };
    }
}

/// The success value a formatting operation with nothing to return produces.
///
/// Spelled once here because it is written at the end of nearly every writer, and `Result::Success<Unit,
/// FormatError>(Unit())` is a great deal of syntax for "that worked".
///
/// Takes nothing. Cannot fail; runs in constant time and allocates nothing.
///
/// @returns a successful result carrying `Unit`
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/format-ok
pub func FormatOk() -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    return Result::Success<Unit, FormatError>(Unit());
}

/// The failure-only kind corresponding to `error`, or `InvalidUtf8` when it reports success.
///
/// The fallback exists because the two types deliberately disagree about whether success is expressible. A caller
/// that might hold `TextError::None` must ask `FromTextError` instead, which answers with a `Result` and so has
/// somewhere to put success.
///
/// @param error the text status to narrow, which must be a failure
/// @returns the matching failure kind
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/text-failure-of
pub func TextFailureOf(error: TextError) -> TextFailureKind {
    if error == TextError::OutOfMemory { return TextFailureKind::OutOfMemory; }
    if error == TextError::LengthOverflow { return TextFailureKind::LengthOverflow; }
    if error == TextError::NotABoundary { return TextFailureKind::NotABoundary; }
    return TextFailureKind::InvalidUtf8;
}

/// `error` translated across the boundary between this package's status convention and the formatting protocol.
///
/// This is the only sanctioned crossing. `TextError::None` becomes `Result::Success`, and every other case becomes
/// a `TextFailure` carrying the reason, so a status never becomes an error payload that means success.
///
/// @param error the text status a storage or validation operation returned
/// @returns success for `None`, and a `TextFailure` carrying the reason otherwise
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/from-text-error
pub func FromTextError(error: TextError) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    if error == TextError::None { return FormatOk(); }
    return Result::Error<Unit, FormatError>(FormatError::TextFailure(TextFailureOf(error)));
}

/// A parsing failure that can say where it happened.
///
/// Every domain owns its own parse error, because what went wrong in a UUID and what went wrong in a timestamp are
/// different questions with different answers, and flattening them into one type would lose the distinctions a
/// caller acts on. What they share is the position, and that is what this names.
///
/// The offset is a zero-based UTF-8 byte offset into the text that was handed to the parser. End of input is the
/// text's length, which is a position and not a failure to have one. A parser embedded in a larger grammar adds the
/// offset of the token it was given, so positions compose.
///
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/parse-failure
pub interface ParseFailure {
    /// Where in the input the failure was found.
    ///
    /// @returns the zero-based UTF-8 byte offset, which is the text's length at end of input
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/parse-failure/offset
    func Offset() -> uint;
}
